<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wuffa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffingas">Wuffingas</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wuffa was the semi-legendary founder of the East Anglian royal house, from whom the dynasty took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffingas">Wuffingas</a>. Later genealogies make him the grandfather of Rædwald and place his rule in the 6th century, but nothing about him is securely known.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wuffa.jpg" alt="Wuffa" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Speed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uffa_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The East Anglian dynastic tally in the Anglian collection names Wuffa as the son of Wehha and the father of Tytila. His name is a diminutive of the Old English word for wolf, and the dynasty's name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffingas">Wuffingas</a>, means literally the kin of Wuffa; the family ruled the East Angles until 749. The kingdom had grown from migrants who arrived in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> from the area now known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, and it was bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, by forests to the south and by the Fens to the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> regarded Wuffa as the first king of the East Angles and called him the grandfather of Rædwald. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>, written a century later, instead names a Guillem Guercha as the first king and Uffa as his son. The 19th-century historian Sir Francis Palgrave held that Guercha was a distortion of Wuffa's own name, but D. P. Kirby concluded that the Historia meant Wehha. The 13th-century chronicler Roger of Wendover has Wuffa ruling from 571 to 578, though the origin of this information is unknown; the historian Michael Wood places his rule around 575.</p><p>It has been suggested that Wuffa's dynasty may have come from a Germanic people known as the Warni, and he has been identified with the King of the Angli whose sister, according to the Byzantine scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, was betrothed to a 6th-century Warni prince called Radigis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>For lack of documentary evidence scholars cannot say whether Wuffa really lived, and the identity of the first East Anglian king cannot be known with certainty. The historian Martin Carver has argued that he is best regarded as an emblematic figure drawn from a royal origin myth. Later East Anglian kings claimed their right to rule through descent from him, as the kings of Kent claimed descent from Oisc. He is believed to have founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> village of Ufford, a name commonly read as Uffa's ford.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wuffa of East Anglia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Angles settled Norfolk and Suffolk in the 5th century, and the Wuffingas dynasty, named after <b>Wuffa</b>, ruled by the 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li></ul></section>
